  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThe upper-level pattern remains benign and is characterized by \na narrow ridge extending from the Gulf toward the Lower Great \nLakes, a cut-off low pressure system over the Four Corners \nregion, and aggregate troughing across the Pacific northwest. \nCloser to the surface, a cold front stretches across the Upper \nMississippi River Valley southeastward from a low pressure \nsystem traversing Hudson Bay. Ahead of the cold front and indeed\nacross our area, it's another seasonably beautiful day with a \nlight southwesterly breeze, partly cloudy skies, and \ntemperatures climbing toward the lower 70s. \n\nThe cold front will drop into northern Illinois and \nnorthwestern Indiana toward daybreak leading to a north to \nnortheasterly wind shift and gusts up to around 15 to 20 mph. \nModest ascent behind the front interacting with the cool and \nmoist environment should support low clouds and even pockets of \nmist/fog immediately behind the front, making way for a fall \nscene to start the workweek. With the parent low pressure system\nremaining so far removed from our area and the aforementioned \nsouthwestern cut-off low expected to lift northeastward toward \nthe northern Plains (inducing low-level warm air advection into \nthe middle Mississippi River Valley), the cold front appears \npoised to stall somewhere between the I-80 and US-24 corridors.\nTaken altogether, the stage is set for a tight gradient in \ntemperatures across the area tomorrow with highs in the low 60s\nnear the Wisconsin state line to the mid 70s south of US-24. \n\nTuesday into Wednesday morning, a surface high pressure system will \nsettle across the northwestern United States as aggregate surface \ntroughing becomes established across the Plains. A series of mid-\nlevel impulses originating from northern Mexico will propagate \nnortheastward toward the Great Lakes atop a somewhat parched low-\nlevel airmass (backside of the surface high). While a sprinkle or \ntwo cannot be ruled out, suspect we'll just have to deal with \nprevalent mid to upper-level clouds leading to a muted diurnal \ntemperature response (highs on Tuesday perhaps only a few \ndegrees warmer than Monday). Clearing skies Wednesday afternoon \nshould help highs warm back into the low 70s areawide. \n\nEnsemble guidance continues to advertise a pattern change toward the \nend of the week and beyond as the subtropical and polar jets begin \nto interact, leading to deep long-wave troughing across the western \nUS and shortwave shedding eastward into the central US. As a result, \nregular chances for rainfall will return to the region perhaps as \nearly as Thursday but more likely toward and after next weekend.\n\nBorchardt",
